title: Act3_Baker
tags: act3 witness_baker
---
You take a deep breath and realize that your hands are trembling.  
The same hands that held many of the people in this room when they were babies.  
That wiped the brows of every mother and soothed every father.


You look around you.


You cannot fathom it.  
The gift of giving life was something you had always worshipped.  
A gift from God.  
A beacon of hope in a time where life was—well… a fight for survival.


But now?  
The faces that once gazed upon you with love and kindness
look at you differently.


All around you are expressions of disgust and resentment.  
Parents pull their children close.  
Women shake their heads and pray.  
Fingers point.


You are vilified.


In everyone’s eyes you have sentenced a child to unbelievable pain and suffering.  
There is nothing worse than betraying your people, and thus betraying God.


There is nothing you can do or say that will matter.  
If you plead guilty, you will die.


But no one pleads guilty.


You look at your darkened, withered skin.  
You feel the beating of your fragile heart beneath your breast.  
To lie about who you were—to betray the faith you lived by—


It is inconceivable.


You are not a coward.  
Rebecca Nurse was not a coward.  
Neither was Mary Eastey or John Proctor.


Steady yourself and reply…


-> "I maintain my innocence above all else."
